+    "conclusions": [
+      "GD-ROM DMA works on this drive and returns the SAME BYTES as PIO: both runs' check passed with identical CRC32 (2d863bc3), and all 8 verify lines matched the PIO reference at both read sizes.",
+      "DMA costs essentially no CPU: the worker used 18-34 ms of a ~4000 ms point (0.4-0.9%), against 3756-3971 ms for PIO (97.6-98.0%). That is the whole point: with PIO the drive read owns the CPU, with DMA it does not.",
+      "DMA is not slower at the size a capture uses: at chunk 128 DMA read 1158.4 KiB/s against PIO's 1156.6; at chunk 32, 1185.5 against 1232.2 (-3.8%), and the DMA timing still includes up to one 10 ms scheduler tick of completion latency.",
+      "A competing thread costs a PIO read 12.7-10.8% but costs a DMA read nothing (+0.9% and -0.3%). So a plain PIO thread split would pay for itself; DMA does not.",
+      "Projected: overlapping the disc read with the SD write and hashing would take a whole-disc capture from 26.7 to about 19.4 minutes (27.6% faster), or about 17.3 with the measured CRC16 and CRC32 replacements as well. An ideal bound, not a measurement of a working design.",
